1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:04,880
They are a glimpse into the future, or a throwback to the past.

2
00:00:05,880 --> 00:00:07,040
They can breathe fire,

3
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:09,000
underwater,

4
00:00:10,200 --> 00:00:11,920
or just go really fast.

5
00:00:15,200 --> 00:00:17,060
Kind of like putting a V8 in a Go-Kat.

6
00:00:17,800 --> 00:00:21,480
They are strange machines built to pose two important questions.

7
00:00:22,380 --> 00:00:23,080
What is it?

8
00:00:23,380 --> 00:00:25,460
And how do I get one?

9
00:00:25,640 --> 00:00:29,000
The easiest description for it is actually that it's kind of like Batman's tool belt.

10
00:00:29,001 --> 00:00:31,600
Join us in discovering today's off-kilter.

11
00:00:32,240 --> 00:00:35,940
On the fringe and out-of-this-world mechanical creations.

12
00:00:36,540 --> 00:00:38,380
And the mad inventors behind them.

13
00:00:39,180 --> 00:00:42,200
Get ready for Weird Machines on Modern Marvels.

14
00:00:42,201 --> 00:00:56,201
It's been said that necessity is the mother of all invention.

15
00:01:01,080 --> 00:01:04,860
But for some inventions, the necessity remains unclear.

16
00:01:06,120 --> 00:01:09,480
There's a growing world of devices resting on the edges of society.

17
00:01:09,481 --> 00:01:14,420
the lone wolves of technology, deviating from the norm,

18
00:01:15,120 --> 00:01:17,040
and all sharing a common attribute.

19
00:01:18,100 --> 00:01:20,700
They're just a touch weird.

20
00:01:24,020 --> 00:01:26,660
We launch right out of the gate in Redding, California,

21
00:01:27,160 --> 00:01:28,900
with a machine whose look says it all.

22
00:01:30,240 --> 00:01:34,240
If you've ever wanted to swim like a shark or dolphin,

23
00:01:34,700 --> 00:01:36,040
well, here's your chance.

24
00:01:36,041 --> 00:01:42,540
Robert Innes and his team at Innes Space Productions have designed the ultimate aquatic thrill

25
00:01:42,541 --> 00:01:51,560
ride, the Sea Breacher X.

26
00:01:51,561 --> 00:01:56,280
This high-octane machine was born out of Innes' goal to build an underwater jet ski.

27
00:01:56,281 --> 00:02:02,520
This boat is about the closest you're going to get to a sensation of a dolphin or a shark.

28
00:02:02,521 --> 00:02:05,060
You go through the water, you jump over it,

29
00:02:05,160 --> 00:02:10,060
you can totally do anything that you can imagine.

30
00:02:10,160 --> 00:02:12,360
This one is our most extreme vehicle.

31
00:02:12,460 --> 00:02:15,160
It puts out about 255 horsepower.

32
00:02:15,260 --> 00:02:19,280
It's capable of about 50 miles an hour on the surface.

33
00:02:19,380 --> 00:02:21,780
If you've got this much power, you can do that.

34
00:02:25,480 --> 00:02:28,680
At the shop, the next fleet of custom-made shark and dolphin

35
00:02:28,780 --> 00:02:31,580
boats are on the line.

36
00:02:31,581 --> 00:02:34,880
The shells and motors arrive prefabricated,

37
00:02:34,881 --> 00:02:35,880
and the work begins.

38
00:02:41,340 --> 00:02:43,660
Welders machine every part based on the specs

39
00:02:43,661 --> 00:02:46,200
of the ordered boat.

40
00:02:46,201 --> 00:02:48,520
It's pretty much where we do all the grinding,

41
00:02:48,521 --> 00:02:53,480
our specific welding, our beginning stages of the fabrication.

42
00:02:53,481 --> 00:02:56,040
This is where it starts.

43
00:02:56,041 --> 00:02:58,460
All the main mechanical components of the sea breacher

44
00:02:58,461 --> 00:03:03,460
are made from non-corrosive stainless steel or aluminum.

45
00:03:03,461 --> 00:03:07,200
We just chop it up, weld it up, and boom, we got parts.

46
00:03:11,300 --> 00:03:14,340
With a base model going for $80,000,

47
00:03:14,341 --> 00:03:18,880
this is definitely not your family's first speedboat.

48
00:03:18,881 --> 00:03:20,980
You know, we have a pretty diverse clientele

49
00:03:20,981 --> 00:03:22,560
of people who buy these.

50
00:03:22,561 --> 00:03:26,780
You know, we have everything from wealthy Arab clients

51
00:03:26,781 --> 00:03:28,260
to red journaling junkies.

52
00:03:29,260 --> 00:03:30,940
And to keep the customers sick,

53
00:03:31,280 --> 00:03:33,960
the sea breacher must remain buoyant at all times.

54
00:03:34,700 --> 00:03:36,320
So within the walls of the sea breacher,

55
00:03:36,400 --> 00:03:38,900
there's actually flotation, which is urethane foam.

56
00:03:39,260 --> 00:03:41,580
So even if the whole cockpit fills with water,

57
00:03:41,880 --> 00:03:43,240
the foam will keep you afloat.

58
00:03:43,620 --> 00:03:45,960
So it's a safety feature built into the fuselage

59
00:03:45,961 --> 00:03:46,740
so it can't sink.

60
00:03:47,380 --> 00:03:48,300
Famous last words.

61
00:03:49,260 --> 00:03:50,560
Just because it won't sink

62
00:03:50,561 --> 00:03:52,300
doesn't mean it can't go underwater.

63
00:03:53,480 --> 00:03:56,020
To replicate the movements of a marine mammal,

64
00:03:56,021 --> 00:04:00,020
Ines had to utilise the mechanics of aerospace engineering.

65
00:04:01,020 --> 00:04:05,020
What we do is we build custom-built submersible watercraft.

66
00:04:05,021 --> 00:04:08,020
These are not submarines. They fly underwater.

67
00:04:08,021 --> 00:04:12,020
So it's really like a cross between a boat and a plane.

68
00:04:15,020 --> 00:04:18,020
Now, a regular aeroplane has wings that suck it up into the sky,

69
00:04:18,021 --> 00:04:21,020
so we use forward momentum and downwards-facing wings

70
00:04:21,021 --> 00:04:23,020
to suck it underneath the water.

71
00:04:23,021 --> 00:04:25,020
If you push these both forward,

72
00:04:25,021 --> 00:04:27,020
it's forcing the entire boat underwater.

73
00:04:27,021 --> 00:04:29,020
It's giving negative lift.

74
00:04:29,021 --> 00:04:31,020
If you pull these both back,

75
00:04:31,021 --> 00:04:33,020
it's actually bringing it up above the surface.

76
00:04:33,021 --> 00:04:36,020
So at different speeds, you're doing different things here.

77
00:04:39,020 --> 00:04:43,020
Sea breacher also requires complex hand and foot coordination

78
00:04:43,021 --> 00:04:45,020
to maximize the watercraft's potential.

79
00:04:45,021 --> 00:04:47,020
You actually do a lot of the controlling

80
00:04:47,021 --> 00:04:49,020
of the sea breacher with your feet here.

81
00:04:49,021 --> 00:04:50,020
It's more like an aircraft.

82
00:04:50,021 --> 00:04:52,020
You push the left pedal to turn left,

83
00:04:52,021 --> 00:04:53,820
right pedal to turn right.

84
00:04:53,821 --> 00:04:56,100
You can also pitch the nose up and down

85
00:04:56,101 --> 00:04:57,820
by pointing your toes forward.

86
00:04:57,821 --> 00:04:59,400
That points it into a dive,

87
00:04:59,401 --> 00:05:00,820
and when you push your heels like that,

88
00:05:00,821 --> 00:05:02,020
that brings the nose up.

89
00:05:02,021 --> 00:05:03,440
That's how you get the big jumps.

90
00:05:07,700 --> 00:05:10,200
But much of the acrobatics performed by the Sea Breacher

91
00:05:10,201 --> 00:05:13,700
stems from a boatload of horsepower.

92
00:05:13,701 --> 00:05:15,080
This particular motor is a three-cylinder.

93
00:05:15,081 --> 00:05:16,420
It's a four-stroke engine,

94
00:05:16,421 --> 00:05:19,540
and it runs on regular gasoline.

95
00:05:19,541 --> 00:05:20,340
Fire it up.

96
00:05:22,540 --> 00:05:25,180
You need a lot of horsepower to get these things under the water.

97
00:05:26,880 --> 00:05:29,360
Typical family ski boat is about 200 horse.

98
00:05:29,720 --> 00:05:33,920
So here we have like a 16-foot canoe with 250 horsepower.

99
00:05:34,460 --> 00:05:36,060
So obviously it's going to haul it.

100
00:05:37,160 --> 00:05:41,120
This fusion of throttle thrust, foot pedal dynamics, and horsepower

101
00:05:41,121 --> 00:05:46,420
allows for a seasoned driver to perform jumps, twists,

102
00:05:47,700 --> 00:05:49,320
and surface 180s.

103
00:05:49,540 --> 00:05:54,720
The coolest thing for me is just flying underwater.

104
00:05:54,721 --> 00:05:57,040
I mean, things happen so quickly.

105
00:05:57,260 --> 00:06:00,020
And people say, you know, you're down there sightseeing, can you see fish?

106
00:06:00,300 --> 00:06:03,380
You just get tunnel vision because everything is happening so quickly.

107
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:06,100
It's kind of like strapping yourself to a torpedo.

108
00:06:11,240 --> 00:06:15,220
But the Sea Breacher isn't the only weird machine sending ripples through the nautical world.

109
00:06:17,300 --> 00:06:19,380
We head to America's final frontier.

110
00:06:19,540 --> 00:06:24,160
where a bizarre watercraft navigates the frigid waters of Juneau, Alaska.

111
00:06:26,540 --> 00:06:30,840
This craft is the Archduke Grand Excelsior, ruler of the high seas.

112
00:06:31,400 --> 00:06:34,760
The Archduke was designed to be an all-weather, go-anywhere craft.

113
00:06:36,460 --> 00:06:42,160
Marine mechanic Cal Giordano single-handedly built the Duke in less than three months in his garage.

114
00:06:43,100 --> 00:06:47,280
Just then one day I thought that, you know, there's no reason why not.

115
00:06:47,360 --> 00:06:48,200
I have the skills.

116
00:06:48,201 --> 00:06:50,800
I work on normal boats all day.

117
00:06:50,801 --> 00:06:52,740
They really don't interest me.

118
00:06:52,741 --> 00:06:57,580
I wanted the boat to be unique, have unique functions,

119
00:06:57,581 --> 00:06:59,720
and just be a different kind of boat.

120
00:07:02,220 --> 00:07:05,260
32 feet in length, the Archduke is a hodgepodge

121
00:07:05,261 --> 00:07:07,080
of commercial and household parts.

122
00:07:08,760 --> 00:07:11,160
A large buoy was chosen for the front of the boat.

123
00:07:13,200 --> 00:07:15,420
A 500 pound propane tank was attached

124
00:07:15,421 --> 00:07:16,820
to counter the weight in the back.

125
00:07:18,200 --> 00:07:20,940
Its look is a cross between a Civil War submarine

126
00:07:20,941 --> 00:07:24,440
and a pirate ship, even donning a cannon on the bow.

127
00:07:28,040 --> 00:07:30,040
To tackle a project of this scale,

128
00:07:30,041 --> 00:07:33,240
one must acquire the right tools of the trade.

129
00:07:33,241 --> 00:07:35,960
Now this is what you need to build the Archduke.

130
00:07:35,961 --> 00:07:37,560
You need a cotton torch.

131
00:07:37,561 --> 00:07:39,960
This is to cut the steel.

132
00:07:39,961 --> 00:07:41,960
You need a grinder.

133
00:07:41,961 --> 00:07:44,360
This is to grind the steel you just cut.

134
00:07:44,361 --> 00:07:45,800
You need a drill.

135
00:07:45,801 --> 00:07:50,800
This is to drill holes in the steel that you just cut and ground.

136
00:07:50,801 --> 00:07:51,800
This is a file.

137
00:07:51,801 --> 00:07:54,800
This is for everything else.

138
00:07:54,801 --> 00:08:00,800
With the hull in place, Giordano went to work converting the commercial pieces into a recreational vehicle.

139
00:08:00,801 --> 00:08:06,800
Now, the Archduke is an unconventional craft made from rather conventional items.

140
00:08:06,801 --> 00:08:08,800
For instance, the buoy here.

141
00:08:08,801 --> 00:08:11,800
This is a 58-inch diameter commercial buoy.

142
00:08:11,801 --> 00:08:14,640
Now, what I've used it for is my command module,

143
00:08:14,740 --> 00:08:17,340
or the cabin where I drive.

144
00:08:17,440 --> 00:08:20,140
We have one-inch thick tempered glass windows.

145
00:08:20,240 --> 00:08:22,680
We have a navy surplus hatch.

146
00:08:22,780 --> 00:08:27,820
I can stand in the middle of this module and turn 360 degrees

147
00:08:27,920 --> 00:08:29,480
for full visibility.

148
00:08:29,580 --> 00:08:32,420
The one-man hub consists of a small steering wheel,

149
00:08:32,520 --> 00:08:35,260
speed throttle, and gauge.

150
00:08:35,360 --> 00:08:37,800
A camera is mounted on top of the boat

151
00:08:37,900 --> 00:08:41,300
and wired to a video monitor inside the cabin.

152
00:08:41,301 --> 00:08:43,300
This is the Archduke's video camera.

153
00:08:43,301 --> 00:08:46,300
When the bow's under, it allows me to see and control the boat

154
00:08:46,301 --> 00:08:48,300
when I can't see through the windshield.

155
00:08:48,301 --> 00:08:52,300
It allows me to see and steer better in rough water.

156
00:08:52,301 --> 00:08:55,300
It allows me to see and drive at night.

157
00:08:57,300 --> 00:09:00,300
The bow is able to dip underwater by using two front wings,

158
00:09:00,301 --> 00:09:03,300
added to mimic the physics of a submarine.

159
00:09:03,301 --> 00:09:07,300
Now this boat, not designed to be a submarine,

160
00:09:07,301 --> 00:09:10,300
but early in its construction I saw that it could easily

161
00:09:10,301 --> 00:09:13,900
it could easily have the capability to be semi-submersible,

162
00:09:14,140 --> 00:09:16,560
which means kind of go underwater.

163
00:09:17,120 --> 00:09:19,100
What we do is we turn this crank.

164
00:09:19,800 --> 00:09:22,180
Now we can see the wings are going lower.

165
00:09:23,040 --> 00:09:24,600
Now with the wings in this position,

166
00:09:24,960 --> 00:09:26,640
as the Archduke moves forward,

167
00:09:27,020 --> 00:09:30,800
these wings dive, pulling the bow underwater along with it.

168
00:09:32,760 --> 00:09:36,280
The Duke is propelled by a single 90-horsepower outboard engine,

169
00:09:36,960 --> 00:09:38,840
traditionally built for small speedboats.

170
00:09:40,300 --> 00:09:43,260
bringing the boat to a top speed of 12 and a half miles an hour.

171
00:09:45,320 --> 00:09:49,340
But with any addition on a boat, new weight should always be split in two.

172
00:09:53,040 --> 00:09:54,940
This is the Archduke's main gun.

173
00:09:55,620 --> 00:10:00,960
This cannon is primarily used as ballast in that it counterweights the outboard engine.

174
00:10:01,400 --> 00:10:04,260
They both weigh about the same, and that keeps the boat level.

175
00:10:05,980 --> 00:10:08,660
The cannon has never fired a live round on the water.

176
00:10:08,661 --> 00:10:13,420
instead relying on a film canister of black powder to simulate a real blast.

177
00:10:18,260 --> 00:10:21,340
However, this may not be the Duke's most unique feature.

178
00:10:22,000 --> 00:10:26,520
One of the most unconventional features of the Archduke is the axle underneath.

179
00:10:27,180 --> 00:10:30,260
Now, the axle is used to move the boat without a trailer.

180
00:10:30,620 --> 00:10:33,040
It's also used to beach the boat and keep it upright.

181
00:10:33,660 --> 00:10:37,380
It allowed me to make the boat basically self-sufficient.

182
00:10:37,381 --> 00:10:40,080
It does not rely on a trailer to move itself.

183
00:10:41,380 --> 00:10:43,560
By welding the axle directly to the hull,

184
00:10:43,960 --> 00:10:45,420
it can be towed right out of the water

185
00:10:45,421 --> 00:10:48,160
with nothing more than a small hitch in a chain.

186
00:10:50,920 --> 00:10:54,160
Giordano relies on a semi-truck to haul the Duke around town.

187
00:10:56,000 --> 00:10:58,400
You don't have to worry about separating them.

188
00:10:59,100 --> 00:11:03,480
It's very convenient knowing that the boat can be hauled on its own.

189
00:11:04,400 --> 00:11:06,400
I'm never going to be captain of a real sub,

190
00:11:06,401 --> 00:11:09,580
but that's not going to stop me from making my own little boat

191
00:11:09,581 --> 00:11:13,740
and driving around, and I just really, really enjoy it.

192
00:11:19,360 --> 00:11:20,940
Weird machines are everywhere

193
00:11:20,941 --> 00:11:23,220
and can arrive in any shape or size.

194
00:11:24,040 --> 00:11:26,160
Some even show practical potential.

195
00:11:28,800 --> 00:11:30,680
Scaling a mountain has become the metaphor

196
00:11:30,681 --> 00:11:33,040
for taking on a nearly impossible task.

197
00:11:33,041 --> 00:11:39,040
The slow, difficult climb to an almost unforeseeable end.

198
00:11:39,041 --> 00:11:42,040
Now, imagine a device that replaces the human effort

199
00:11:42,041 --> 00:11:45,040
completely, allowing you to literally dance up a rock

200
00:11:45,041 --> 00:11:46,040
surface.

201
00:11:49,040 --> 00:11:53,040
Atlas Devices has built a machine enabling you to do just that.

202
00:11:56,040 --> 00:12:00,040
No bigger than a large shoebox, the power sender is a reverse

203
00:12:00,041 --> 00:12:02,440
The core sender is a reverse repeller

204
00:12:02,441 --> 00:12:05,540
capable of hauling over 20 times its own way.

205
00:12:05,541 --> 00:12:07,080
The easiest description for it is actually

206
00:12:07,081 --> 00:12:09,040
that it's kind of like Batman's tool belt.

207
00:12:09,041 --> 00:12:11,980
It's literally the part that pulls you up the rope.

208
00:12:11,981 --> 00:12:13,680
We've got a big battery here,

209
00:12:13,681 --> 00:12:14,780
motor in your gearbox,

210
00:12:14,781 --> 00:12:17,500
and an electronic control unit that controls the speed.

211
00:12:17,501 --> 00:12:21,500
So I can just clip with any standard carometer,

212
00:12:21,501 --> 00:12:23,900
and I'm ready to climb.

213
00:12:23,901 --> 00:12:27,040
So once it's threaded, I can adjust the speed.

214
00:12:27,041 --> 00:12:29,340
I can climb very, very slowly,

215
00:12:29,341 --> 00:12:32,980
and then when I descend, it releases the brake.

216
00:12:32,981 --> 00:12:35,340
If I let go, hands-free any time,

217
00:12:35,341 --> 00:12:36,720
I'm gonna stay there in place.

218
00:12:36,721 --> 00:12:40,220
If I wanna go up, I just adjust that variable speed trigger

219
00:12:40,221 --> 00:12:41,260
and can go slow,

220
00:12:42,960 --> 00:12:44,260
or I can move pretty quick.

221
00:12:48,360 --> 00:12:51,300
In 2004, a group of MIT students

222
00:12:51,301 --> 00:12:53,400
who later started ATLAS devices

223
00:12:53,401 --> 00:12:54,940
were enlisted by the Army

224
00:12:54,941 --> 00:12:58,400
to build a new climbing instrument for tactical missions.

225
00:12:58,401 --> 00:13:00,200
Get up here with this one right here.

226
00:13:00,201 --> 00:13:01,700
They were in need of a device that

227
00:13:01,701 --> 00:13:04,280
would allow for quick ascents across the mountainous

228
00:13:04,281 --> 00:13:06,900
regions of Afghanistan.

229
00:13:06,901 --> 00:13:09,640
So they wanted something roughly the size of a shoebox.

230
00:13:09,641 --> 00:13:12,920
It weighs about 25 pounds to lift guys up and down in the air.

231
00:13:12,921 --> 00:13:15,320
And this is that prototype that we first built.

232
00:13:18,580 --> 00:13:20,680
The students developed the device

233
00:13:20,681 --> 00:13:23,760
by following the mechanics of a common household tool.

234
00:13:23,761 --> 00:13:26,160
It's kind of inspired by a power drill.

235
00:13:26,161 --> 00:13:27,900
There's a handle or a grip right here,

236
00:13:27,901 --> 00:13:29,140
here by my finger.

237
00:13:29,141 --> 00:13:31,200
And when I squeeze this red button,

238
00:13:31,201 --> 00:13:34,900
it turns on the system, and then it rides up and down the rope.

239
00:13:34,901 --> 00:13:36,740
So instead of sort of winding the rope,

240
00:13:36,741 --> 00:13:39,320
maybe like a normal winch would, this actually goes up

241
00:13:39,321 --> 00:13:40,280
and down the rope.

242
00:13:40,281 --> 00:13:43,780
So the rope becomes a highway.

243
00:13:43,781 --> 00:13:45,980
In addition to helping troops in the field,

244
00:13:45,981 --> 00:13:47,580
the Office of Naval Research also

245
00:13:47,581 --> 00:13:50,120
foresees potential in the ascender for maritime

246
00:13:50,121 --> 00:13:53,720
and rescue operations.

247
00:13:53,721 --> 00:13:55,700
By threading rope through a litter and the harness

248
00:13:55,701 --> 00:13:58,700
of the rescuer, the device can pull the weight of two people

249
00:13:58,701 --> 00:14:00,040
out of a compromising area.

250
00:14:01,600 --> 00:14:03,600
So in other words, that could become a one-man rescue

251
00:14:03,601 --> 00:14:06,800
operation where now we would have to use two or three people

252
00:14:06,801 --> 00:14:07,760
in the same situation.

253
00:14:08,220 --> 00:14:09,720
I can do the same thing from a helicopter.

254
00:14:11,860 --> 00:14:14,500
Traditionally, heli-rescue efforts require aircrafts

255
00:14:14,501 --> 00:14:17,420
with specialized winches to airlift endangered personnel.

256
00:14:18,880 --> 00:14:21,700
The Ascender has the potential to convert any helicopter

257
00:14:21,701 --> 00:14:22,860
into a rescue vehicle.

258
00:14:22,861 --> 00:14:26,200
with its ability to lift hundreds of pounds at once.

259
00:14:28,340 --> 00:14:30,880
At the Atlas headquarters in Boston, Massachusetts,

260
00:14:31,640 --> 00:14:33,620
a weight verification test is in progress

261
00:14:33,621 --> 00:14:35,560
to ensure its maximum weight capacity.

262
00:14:36,640 --> 00:14:39,480
Right now I have the ascender attached to 500 pounds

263
00:14:39,481 --> 00:14:40,580
of just pure weight,

264
00:14:40,820 --> 00:14:43,040
and this is well above what it's actually rated for.

265
00:14:43,300 --> 00:14:45,000
It gives us an opportunity to make sure

266
00:14:45,001 --> 00:14:46,980
that the system can do not only what it's supposed to do,

267
00:14:46,981 --> 00:14:49,320
but also get a little bit extra when the customer needs it.

268
00:14:49,321 --> 00:14:52,820
The reason the power to ascender is actually able to pull so

269
00:14:52,821 --> 00:14:56,320
much weight with a battery is really due to a very highly

270
00:14:56,321 --> 00:14:58,320
integrated mechanical, electromechanical design

271
00:14:58,321 --> 00:15:00,320
in the system.

272
00:15:00,321 --> 00:15:03,320
Beyond the proprietary design,

273
00:15:03,321 --> 00:15:06,320
the power to mass ratio technology enables a small

274
00:15:06,321 --> 00:15:09,320
battery to lift enormous amounts of weight.

275
00:15:09,321 --> 00:15:13,320
The ability of a standard charge depends on the weight being

276
00:15:13,321 --> 00:15:14,320
lifted.

277
00:15:14,321 --> 00:15:17,320
As the pounds increase, the length and speed the ascender can

278
00:15:17,321 --> 00:15:19,820
the ascender can travel decreases.

279
00:15:19,821 --> 00:15:24,320
The less the weight, the faster you can skim up a rock surface.

280
00:15:24,321 --> 00:15:26,320
The ascender weighs roughly 26 pounds

281
00:15:26,321 --> 00:15:29,860
compared to 500 pounds, 1 20th of what it's lifting.

282
00:15:29,861 --> 00:15:31,360
It's pretty incredible.

283
00:15:31,361 --> 00:15:33,360
So it's quite a power tool.

284
00:15:35,480 --> 00:15:39,360
The sensation of moving upward at walking to running speed

285
00:15:39,361 --> 00:15:42,360
along a vertical surface is just, it's really out there.

286
00:15:42,361 --> 00:15:43,980
Very fun.

287
00:15:43,981 --> 00:15:45,360
The power ascender's goal

288
00:15:45,361 --> 00:15:48,520
Ascender's goal is to alleviate vertical obstacles,

289
00:15:48,521 --> 00:15:51,400
while this next mechanical oddity is destined to ease

290
00:15:51,401 --> 00:15:52,900
the efforts of everyday life.

291
00:15:56,060 --> 00:15:58,820
What could be weirder, yet more appealing,

292
00:15:58,821 --> 00:16:02,820
than to have your own personal robot at your service

293
00:16:02,821 --> 00:16:08,860
to greet you at the door, open a window,

294
00:16:08,861 --> 00:16:12,860
even fill in as a workout partner?

295
00:16:12,861 --> 00:16:15,360
While this futuristic automaton may not be ready

296
00:16:15,361 --> 00:16:19,440
for domestic use, it does have a special role

297
00:16:19,441 --> 00:16:21,360
within the realm of the US military.

298
00:16:25,540 --> 00:16:28,040
Currently in development at Vecna Technologies,

299
00:16:28,041 --> 00:16:31,340
BEAR, or Battlefield Extraction Assist Robot,

300
00:16:31,341 --> 00:16:34,540
is the 21st century knight in shining armor.

301
00:16:34,541 --> 00:16:38,320
This is the world's most powerful humanoid robot.

302
00:16:38,321 --> 00:16:41,920
Designed to engage in casualty rescue, bomb disposal,

303
00:16:41,921 --> 00:16:44,560
and perform all-around workhorse tasks,

304
00:16:44,561 --> 00:16:48,320
this robot demanded an extremely robust operating platform,

305
00:16:50,120 --> 00:16:51,560
a tall order made possible

306
00:16:51,561 --> 00:16:54,160
through a complex electrical and hydraulic system.

307
00:16:55,040 --> 00:16:58,400
The bear is the Hercules of humanoid robotics.

308
00:16:58,401 --> 00:17:01,040
The way hydraulics works is it takes electrical energy

309
00:17:01,041 --> 00:17:02,480
and turns a pump.

310
00:17:02,481 --> 00:17:04,800
That pump builds up high pressure,

311
00:17:04,801 --> 00:17:07,040
hundreds of times greater than atmospheric pressure,

312
00:17:07,041 --> 00:17:09,240
and then we use valves to release that pressure

313
00:17:09,241 --> 00:17:11,360
to all the different joints.

314
00:17:11,361 --> 00:17:16,240
The system creates so much strength that each finger alone can lift over 100 pounds.

315
00:17:19,040 --> 00:17:23,200
Beyond its brute force, the hydraulics provide the joint articulation needed

316
00:17:23,201 --> 00:17:26,400
when transporting humans or handling complicated bomb removals.

317
00:17:27,760 --> 00:17:30,745
The bare arms each have six degrees of freedom.

318
00:17:30,746 --> 00:17:33,920
We have a shoulder, an elbow, and a wrist. Each of

319
00:17:33,921 --> 00:17:35,965
those can move in two different ways.

320
00:17:35,966 --> 00:17:39,280
That gives us a lot of flexibility on how to pick things up.

321
00:17:39,281 --> 00:17:42,580
There's not just one way to reach out and grab onto an object.

322
00:17:42,581 --> 00:17:45,580
There are multiple ways.

323
00:17:45,581 --> 00:17:48,080
The idea is that the bear has to be very, very flexible

324
00:17:48,081 --> 00:17:50,320
because you don't know what you're going to run into.

325
00:17:50,321 --> 00:17:52,920
It doesn't know what obstacles are going to be in the way.

326
00:17:52,921 --> 00:17:55,720
It doesn't know what objects it's going to have to move.

327
00:17:55,721 --> 00:17:58,140
So the goal was to design something that's very capable.

328
00:18:00,840 --> 00:18:02,140
And bear's look is fashioned

329
00:18:02,141 --> 00:18:04,840
to also enhance that capability.

330
00:18:04,841 --> 00:18:06,180
In a rescue situation,

331
00:18:06,181 --> 00:18:09,280
assessing the mental state of the victim is imperative.

332
00:18:09,281 --> 00:18:12,880
Vecna designed the face in a way to exude a calming sense,

333
00:18:12,881 --> 00:18:16,720
even provoke laughter to ease a victim's agitated condition.

334
00:18:18,160 --> 00:18:21,920
Cameras within the eye sockets are designed to take commands from hand gestures,

335
00:18:22,460 --> 00:18:25,220
as well as record information for future analysis,

336
00:18:26,400 --> 00:18:28,800
an invaluable application for combat extractions.

337
00:18:31,300 --> 00:18:35,360
The team beyond the Bear robot has its sights beyond tactical command,

338
00:18:35,361 --> 00:18:39,760
slowly gaining traction to one day becoming a household fixture.

339
00:18:40,760 --> 00:18:46,460
When the word robot was invented, it was invented to describe a general purpose worker.

340
00:18:47,380 --> 00:18:50,400
I believe that the future will be a future full of robots.

341
00:18:55,280 --> 00:18:58,520
The machine rose out of the need to simplify human tasks.

342
00:19:00,160 --> 00:19:02,520
But there's one built to do just the opposite.

343
00:19:02,521 --> 00:19:07,360
by converting a simple task into a highly complex one.

344
00:19:10,260 --> 00:19:11,900
Los Angeles, California.

345
00:19:12,320 --> 00:19:15,820
The rock band OK Go is crafting its latest music video

346
00:19:15,821 --> 00:19:18,400
based on the popular invention cartoon series

347
00:19:18,401 --> 00:19:19,820
by artist Rube Goldberg.

348
00:19:20,380 --> 00:19:22,300
If you want the smaller ones, you've got smaller.

349
00:19:23,020 --> 00:19:23,900
No, this is perfect.

350
00:19:23,901 --> 00:19:24,601
Yeah.

351
00:19:24,602 --> 00:19:27,220
The video treatment calls for a massive chain reaction

352
00:19:27,221 --> 00:19:29,620
sequence to be constructed, better known

353
00:19:29,621 --> 00:19:31,100
as a Rube Goldberg machine.

354
00:19:32,520 --> 00:19:34,480
Does it always jump in the same area?

355
00:19:34,481 --> 00:19:37,960
It seems to be jumping on this area, not up here.

356
00:19:38,260 --> 00:19:41,480
A Rube Goldberg invention is something that's important because it's not important.

357
00:19:41,860 --> 00:19:48,720
It's a device that takes something that is really simple, really easy to do in your everyday life, and make it really complex.

358
00:19:49,960 --> 00:19:55,300
For example, here's a Goldberg machine built especially for us to power up a television.

359
00:19:55,780 --> 00:19:59,500
The rat trap knocks the wheel forward, sending the CO2 rocket into the pendulum,

360
00:19:59,501 --> 00:20:01,540
which travels down and closes the pyramid,

361
00:20:01,541 --> 00:20:03,200
forcing Stonehenge to rise,

362
00:20:03,201 --> 00:20:05,500
pushing the mallet to fall on the spatula,

363
00:20:05,501 --> 00:20:08,000
pulls the string, activating the gumball machine,

364
00:20:08,001 --> 00:20:10,040
kicking the tire that rolls to the cannon,

365
00:20:10,041 --> 00:20:11,240
shooting the shot point,

366
00:20:11,241 --> 00:20:14,040
which funnels down the catcher rig, depressing the hand,

367
00:20:14,041 --> 00:20:16,660
completing the task of turning on the TV.

368
00:20:19,660 --> 00:20:24,160
What if this quick release were on a pole here or something?

369
00:20:24,161 --> 00:20:26,600
But for the band, building a Goldberg machine

370
00:20:26,601 --> 00:20:28,560
is one facet of a larger vision.

371
00:20:29,500 --> 00:20:32,340
Each step must coincide with particular song cues

372
00:20:32,440 --> 00:20:34,900
throughout the four-minute running time.

373
00:20:35,000 --> 00:20:37,140
The line that is sung in there is not

374
00:20:37,240 --> 00:20:39,140
going to happen until tink, tink, tink, tink is done,

375
00:20:39,240 --> 00:20:40,040
no matter what.

376
00:20:42,540 --> 00:20:44,140
The band's performance will be woven

377
00:20:44,240 --> 00:20:47,160
throughout the machine's operation.

378
00:20:47,260 --> 00:20:49,860
And of course, all of it has to be captured on camera

379
00:20:49,960 --> 00:20:51,560
in one continuous shot.

380
00:20:54,960 --> 00:20:57,100
This entanglement of machinery and timing

381
00:20:57,101 --> 00:20:59,480
is at the core of Rube Goldberg's work.

382
00:21:01,400 --> 00:21:04,900
After graduating with a degree in engineering in 1904,

383
00:21:04,901 --> 00:21:06,960
Rube Goldberg quickly changed gears

384
00:21:06,961 --> 00:21:08,780
to pursue a career in cartooning.

385
00:21:09,960 --> 00:21:13,560
He soon began penning comics for the San Francisco Chronicle,

386
00:21:13,561 --> 00:21:16,760
lampooning the changing times of early 20th century life.

387
00:21:19,020 --> 00:21:23,240
In 1914, he debuted his satirical Invention series,

388
00:21:23,241 --> 00:21:25,600
greatly influenced by his engineering background.

389
00:21:27,100 --> 00:21:31,100
Goldberg described his inventions as symbols of man's capacity

390
00:21:31,101 --> 00:21:34,100
for exerting maximum effort to accomplish minimum results.

391
00:21:34,101 --> 00:21:39,100
A typical invention consisted of several household items,

392
00:21:39,101 --> 00:21:43,100
including animals, linked together to accomplish a common goal,

393
00:21:43,101 --> 00:21:46,100
such as a haircut or self-operating napkin.

394
00:21:46,101 --> 00:21:50,100
His cartoons were particularly popular during the machine era,

395
00:21:50,101 --> 00:21:54,100
where lots of people were going out and trying to invent machines

396
00:21:54,101 --> 00:21:56,600
to solve any problem they possibly could.

397
00:21:56,601 --> 00:21:59,600
So Rube Goldberg was really making fun of the fact that,

398
00:21:59,601 --> 00:22:02,440
okay, we can design all this technology,

399
00:22:02,441 --> 00:22:04,640
but does it really make our lives simpler?

400
00:22:04,641 --> 00:22:07,140
That's left up to the reader.

401
00:22:07,141 --> 00:22:09,140
During his 70-year career,

402
00:22:09,141 --> 00:22:12,260
Goldberg produced over 50,000 cartoons,

403
00:22:12,261 --> 00:22:17,060
but his legacy will always be linked to his weird machines.

404
00:22:17,061 --> 00:22:21,260
I'm hoping we can get past the first 15 seconds of the song.

405
00:22:21,261 --> 00:22:22,600
Okay. I got it.

406
00:22:22,601 --> 00:22:25,440
Three, two, one, action.

407
00:22:33,340 --> 00:22:34,920
Wall still got caught?

408
00:22:34,921 --> 00:22:36,840
Everybody got it.

409
00:22:36,841 --> 00:22:39,580
Back in Los Angeles, the OK Go video crew

410
00:22:39,581 --> 00:22:43,260
has gone through several takes with little success.

411
00:22:43,261 --> 00:22:45,920
The precision required to execute the machine's steps

412
00:22:45,921 --> 00:22:48,260
is almost immeasurable.

413
00:22:48,261 --> 00:22:50,260
Anything a fraction of an inch off

414
00:22:50,261 --> 00:22:52,660
can bring the entire machine to a screeching halt.

415
00:22:56,160 --> 00:22:58,900
Every year, engineering students across the country

416
00:22:59,000 --> 00:23:02,800
compete in Purdue University's Rube Goldberg Contest.

417
00:23:02,900 --> 00:23:05,740
It's a celebration of gadgetry, with students concocting

418
00:23:05,840 --> 00:23:09,480
the ultimate Goldbergian machines.

419
00:23:09,580 --> 00:23:11,980
So the competition has a certain set of rules, which

420
00:23:12,080 --> 00:23:13,620
everyone has to abide by.

421
00:23:13,720 --> 00:23:16,520
Some of it is the machine has to be built six foot by six foot

422
00:23:16,620 --> 00:23:17,420
by six foot.

423
00:23:17,520 --> 00:23:19,620
No live animals can be used in the machine.

424
00:23:19,621 --> 00:23:21,760
No dead animals can be used in the machine.

425
00:23:21,860 --> 00:23:24,320
No fire and no explosions, and nothing is allowed

426
00:23:24,420 --> 00:23:25,420
to leave the machine.

427
00:23:25,520 --> 00:23:28,860
Other than that, as long as you don't injure or hurt anybody,

428
00:23:28,960 --> 00:23:30,960
have fun.

429
00:23:31,060 --> 00:23:33,160
The same task is assigned to each team,

430
00:23:33,260 --> 00:23:38,580
such as turning on a flashlight or dispensing hand sanitizer

431
00:23:38,680 --> 00:23:41,980
to be done in no fewer than 20 steps.

432
00:23:42,080 --> 00:23:44,620
Known as the Engineering Olympics of America,

433
00:23:44,720 --> 00:23:46,820
these Goldberg machines now incorporate

434
00:23:46,821 --> 00:23:49,860
over 200 separate steps.

435
00:23:49,960 --> 00:23:52,060
We worked 2,000 hours on these machines,

436
00:23:52,160 --> 00:23:54,620
and right down to the minute before we run it,

437
00:23:54,720 --> 00:23:56,460
these things might still be finicky.

438
00:23:56,560 --> 00:24:00,460
Luck, the humidity, the given day where the sun is aligned,

439
00:24:00,560 --> 00:24:03,940
everything plays a major factor in how these things operate.

440
00:24:04,040 --> 00:24:05,480
Ironically, over the 50 years

441
00:24:05,580 --> 00:24:07,340
he drew the invention cartoon,

442
00:24:07,440 --> 00:24:09,940
Goldberg never constructed one himself.

443
00:24:10,040 --> 00:24:12,020
Rube Goldberg never built his inventions,

444
00:24:12,120 --> 00:24:14,180
and really, he never intended them to be built.

445
00:24:14,181 --> 00:24:17,980
His goal was just to draw cartoons that were funny

446
00:24:18,080 --> 00:24:20,520
and would help people laugh at their everyday lives.

447
00:24:23,280 --> 00:24:27,020
And way back.

448
00:24:27,120 --> 00:24:29,320
Returning to the video shoot in LA,

449
00:24:29,420 --> 00:24:31,640
production has moved into day two,

450
00:24:31,740 --> 00:24:35,040
as the crews work to execute one seamless Goldberg sequence.

451
00:24:37,840 --> 00:24:42,040
Closing in on their 85th take, the production team

452
00:24:42,041 --> 00:24:44,740
believes they have problem solved every bug out of the machine.

453
00:24:44,741 --> 00:24:48,280
Three, two, one, action.

454
00:24:48,281 --> 00:24:52,780
A tap of a domino ignites the chain reaction,

455
00:24:52,781 --> 00:24:56,480
cueing the music along the way.

456
00:24:56,481 --> 00:24:59,500
OK Go's Goldberg machine includes over 50 steps

457
00:24:59,501 --> 00:25:00,500
to accomplish its task.

458
00:25:04,300 --> 00:25:07,160
Each band member appears at four separate locations

459
00:25:07,161 --> 00:25:08,600
throughout the single take video.

460
00:25:12,040 --> 00:25:23,540
The sequence unfolds over two levels of the building.

461
00:25:39,903 --> 00:25:45,800
And in the final seconds of the song, a fusillade of gadgetry rips across the room.

462
00:25:47,800 --> 00:25:52,020
And all of this built to trigger a firing range of tape cannons.

463
00:25:53,540 --> 00:26:03,800
What constitutes a weird machine?

464
00:26:04,380 --> 00:26:08,040
Is it the shell of the device or the function underneath?

465
00:26:09,880 --> 00:26:13,380
Steampunk is a subgenre that believes it must be both,

466
00:26:13,840 --> 00:26:16,340
fusing sculptural design with mechanical engineering.

467
00:26:17,120 --> 00:26:19,660
From light fixtures and small hot engines

468
00:26:19,661 --> 00:26:22,600
to cars and even provocative gas masks,

469
00:26:22,601 --> 00:26:26,600
steampunk brings history, art form, and moving parts

470
00:26:26,601 --> 00:26:29,600
all into one weird piece.

471
00:26:29,601 --> 00:26:31,600
The term steam is used to pay homage

472
00:26:31,601 --> 00:26:33,600
to the look and mechanics of the steam engine

473
00:26:33,601 --> 00:26:36,600
during the Victorian era of the 1800s,

474
00:26:36,601 --> 00:26:40,600
a time known as the Age of Invention,

475
00:26:40,601 --> 00:26:46,600
while punk symbolizes the lone wolf do-it-yourself engineer.

476
00:26:46,601 --> 00:26:49,600
The importance of the Victorian era to steampunks

477
00:26:49,601 --> 00:26:52,600
is that the Victorian era had all of the things

478
00:26:52,601 --> 00:26:54,708
famous scientists and inventors.

479
00:26:54,709 --> 00:26:58,660
They were creating everything from gramophones to locomotive

480
00:26:58,661 --> 00:27:02,980
engines to the electric light bulb.

481
00:27:02,981 --> 00:27:08,100
Today's steampunks utilize oil, propane gas, and even the heat of candles to fuel their

482
00:27:08,101 --> 00:27:08,801
machines.

483
00:27:08,802 --> 00:27:13,020
Much of the metal is conditioned to appear aged, as if it came right out of

484
00:27:13,021 --> 00:27:20,920
Thomas Edison's workshop.

485
00:27:20,921 --> 00:27:23,560
and engineer John Saragardi has found his niche

486
00:27:23,660 --> 00:27:25,720
in the steampunk world.

487
00:27:25,820 --> 00:27:29,360
At his Oakland, California-based company, Form and Reform,

488
00:27:29,460 --> 00:27:34,760
he specializes in the ultimate punk machine, the art car.

489
00:27:34,860 --> 00:27:36,860
These are weird machines.

490
00:27:36,960 --> 00:27:38,980
These are not things you would see in the shopping mall

491
00:27:39,080 --> 00:27:41,980
or driving down the street on your way to work.

492
00:27:45,380 --> 00:27:48,220
His largest piece is the Golden Mean,

493
00:27:48,221 --> 00:27:51,060
Or to onlookers, the snail car.

494
00:27:51,061 --> 00:27:52,760
This is the Golden Mean.

495
00:27:52,761 --> 00:27:59,160
It's a giant iron snail car made on a 1966-67 VW Bug.

496
00:27:59,161 --> 00:28:01,760
This was a honeydew project from my wife,

497
00:28:01,761 --> 00:28:04,860
who wanted a giant snail.

498
00:28:04,861 --> 00:28:07,900
Built to California Department of Motor Vehicles standard,

499
00:28:07,901 --> 00:28:09,740
the Golden Mean is street legal,

500
00:28:09,741 --> 00:28:12,420
with a top speed of 55 miles an hour.

501
00:28:15,240 --> 00:28:16,940
Oh, and one more thing.

502
00:28:16,941 --> 00:28:18,140
It breathes fire.

503
00:28:28,660 --> 00:28:31,520
Starting with just the chassis and engine of the VW Bug,

504
00:28:32,060 --> 00:28:34,680
Sarigardi built the snail's frame in custom designs.

505
00:28:35,920 --> 00:28:38,800
To gain the look and feel of a true steampunk aesthetic,

506
00:28:39,380 --> 00:28:41,780
he went back to a traditional form of metalworking.

507
00:28:42,540 --> 00:28:45,140
I incorporate a lot of blacksmithing into the work that I do.

508
00:28:45,141 --> 00:28:48,480
I like the organic feel that it gives, a hand-touched.

509
00:28:48,580 --> 00:28:50,720
You can tell that it's not just come out of the processing

510
00:28:50,820 --> 00:28:52,620
machine.

511
00:28:52,720 --> 00:28:54,620
It gives my work an edge.

512
00:28:54,720 --> 00:28:56,580
It's not just fabricated.

513
00:28:56,680 --> 00:29:00,660
There's also some really great handwork in it.

514
00:29:00,760 --> 00:29:04,020
In total, Sara Gardie added over 2,000 pounds of new sheet

515
00:29:04,120 --> 00:29:07,600
metal and steel to the existing chassis.

516
00:29:07,700 --> 00:29:10,800
The snail car was outfitted with an air ride system.

517
00:29:10,900 --> 00:29:13,740
A flip of a switch will raise or lower the car to adjust

518
00:29:13,741 --> 00:29:14,940
to the number of passengers.

519
00:29:14,941 --> 00:29:16,500
Depending on how many people I have in here,

520
00:29:16,501 --> 00:29:17,980
I've had up to 19 people.

521
00:29:17,981 --> 00:29:22,380
I can turn up to 120 psi and still maintain suspension.

522
00:29:22,381 --> 00:29:24,780
And about those blame-throwing eyes.

523
00:29:24,781 --> 00:29:25,780
Fire.

524
00:29:25,781 --> 00:29:27,580
One of the big features of the Snail ARC car

525
00:29:27,581 --> 00:29:29,780
is fire coming out of its eye stalks.

526
00:29:29,781 --> 00:29:32,500
We have a liquid fuel propane source.

527
00:29:32,501 --> 00:29:34,900
This fuel is boiled off at room temperature

528
00:29:34,901 --> 00:29:38,340
and converted to gas, which we're storing inside of here.

529
00:29:38,341 --> 00:29:42,140
I have a valve that I can control that gas when it comes out.

530
00:29:42,141 --> 00:29:44,940
By pulling on that, it releases gas out the top.

531
00:29:44,980 --> 00:29:48,140
I have a separate system that's feeding the pilot light

532
00:29:48,180 --> 00:29:50,980
that's up here a needle valve to control how much gas

533
00:29:51,020 --> 00:29:52,380
I want out of that.

534
00:29:52,420 --> 00:29:54,580
The tank is housed in the neck of the snail,

535
00:29:54,620 --> 00:29:57,220
with leather reins attached to the gas valve.

536
00:29:57,260 --> 00:29:59,660
Pull on these.

537
00:29:59,700 --> 00:30:01,100
We got fireballs.

538
00:30:01,120 --> 00:30:04,900
This rig launches fireballs up to 16 feet in the air,

539
00:30:04,920 --> 00:30:08,300
a steampunk's visceral ode to the legendary machines of yore.

540
00:30:08,301 --> 00:30:14,300
Each year, art cars such as the Golden Meath make a pilgrimage

541
00:30:14,301 --> 00:30:16,300
to the vast open roads of Burning Man.

542
00:30:19,300 --> 00:30:22,300
Nestled in northern Nevada, the 10-day retreat unfolds

543
00:30:22,301 --> 00:30:25,300
within the Black Rock Desert's 400 square miles

544
00:30:25,301 --> 00:30:27,300
of arid, hardened earth.

545
00:30:30,300 --> 00:30:32,300
The Burning Man project has become a mecca for the weird

546
00:30:32,301 --> 00:30:36,300
and creative, allowing for tens of thousands of people

547
00:30:36,301 --> 00:30:41,300
to showcase their strange toys and machines.

548
00:30:41,400 --> 00:30:43,880
Every August, artists arrive with their latest

549
00:30:43,980 --> 00:30:50,740
performance vehicles, weird cars, and art installations.

550
00:30:50,840 --> 00:30:53,680
The enormous desert backdrop serves as the ultimate arena

551
00:30:53,780 --> 00:30:55,620
to celebrate these gargantuan machines.

552
00:30:59,060 --> 00:31:01,720
And when the sun goes down, another weird machine

553
00:31:01,820 --> 00:31:06,300
emerges to captivate the Burning Man audience.

554
00:31:06,301 --> 00:31:18,260
California-based performance group Omega Recoil has plugged into the high voltage converter

555
00:31:18,261 --> 00:31:22,060
known as the Tesla coil.

556
00:31:22,061 --> 00:31:26,880
Born from the genius mind of Nikola Tesla in the late 1800s, this electrical oddity

557
00:31:26,881 --> 00:31:32,980
still baffles its audiences over 100 years after it was invented.

558
00:31:32,981 --> 00:31:38,440
The device acts as a transformer using magnetically resonating coils.

559
00:31:38,441 --> 00:31:44,040
As energy is transferred from the primary to secondary coil, the voltage is multiplied,

560
00:31:44,041 --> 00:31:51,860
causing extremely high voltage bursts of electricity to emit from the discharge rank.

561
00:31:51,861 --> 00:31:56,520
John Behrens of Omega Recoil helped build a mini Tesla coil to better demonstrate how

562
00:31:56,521 --> 00:32:00,920
it works and to study the behavior of electricity.

563
00:32:00,921 --> 00:32:02,760
And in essence, we're simulating lightning

564
00:32:02,761 --> 00:32:04,820
by using the Tesla coil to create a high enough voltage

565
00:32:04,821 --> 00:32:07,660
that it will jump through the air.

566
00:32:07,661 --> 00:32:10,400
The effect of the arc that we see

567
00:32:10,401 --> 00:32:14,200
is electrons trying to find their way to ground.

568
00:32:14,201 --> 00:32:17,300
And in the process of that, ionizing the atmosphere

569
00:32:17,301 --> 00:32:19,100
and creating little arc trails.

570
00:32:22,500 --> 00:32:24,680
The coil's potential can be best demonstrated

571
00:32:24,681 --> 00:32:27,180
using a simple fluorescent tube.

572
00:32:27,181 --> 00:32:28,840
Usually, you have to have this connected

573
00:32:28,841 --> 00:32:30,540
to an electrical circuit.

574
00:32:30,541 --> 00:32:32,780
The coil itself creates a field.

575
00:32:39,360 --> 00:32:42,160
That just causes a delight by the proximity of it.

576
00:32:42,880 --> 00:32:44,320
With this weird machine,

577
00:32:44,760 --> 00:32:47,620
Tesla believed he could wirelessly transmit power globally,

578
00:32:48,120 --> 00:32:49,420
using the Earth as the conductor.

579
00:32:50,220 --> 00:32:52,440
Though this plan was never fully realized,

580
00:32:53,340 --> 00:32:55,900
the coil did lead to wireless radio technology.

581
00:32:55,901 --> 00:33:01,240
And today, it lives on in a brilliant spectacle of life.

582
00:33:05,720 --> 00:33:11,420
Barron's and the Omega recoil team bring theatrics to Tesla's invention using nine-foot coils,

583
00:33:12,080 --> 00:33:18,640
producing 14-foot arcs that range anywhere between 500,000 and 1 million volts of electricity.

584
00:33:19,100 --> 00:33:21,160
How does a guy end up in this situation?

585
00:33:21,720 --> 00:33:25,780
A performer wears a Faraday suit, which is made of steel and copper.

586
00:33:25,900 --> 00:33:31,400
This conductive shield allows for the electricity to skate across the top of the metal as it

587
00:33:31,401 --> 00:33:36,420
heads to ground, leaving the performer unscathed.

588
00:33:36,421 --> 00:33:41,300
Anything that is not metal, or a poor conductor of electricity, will burn or catch fire as

589
00:33:41,301 --> 00:33:43,500
the current passes through it.

590
00:33:43,501 --> 00:33:46,620
The result?

591
00:33:46,621 --> 00:33:51,740
A symphony of electricity, and a fitting tribute to a man's dream of illuminating the world.

592
00:33:55,900 --> 00:33:59,100
Our journey in discovering eccentric machinery now takes

593
00:33:59,200 --> 00:34:02,540
us deep into the woods of Durham Town, Georgia,

594
00:34:02,640 --> 00:34:05,440
where a unique set of wheels is shredding up

595
00:34:05,540 --> 00:34:06,740
the backcountry trails.

596
00:34:10,340 --> 00:34:14,760
The motocross board, or MXB, is a hybrid breakthrough

597
00:34:14,860 --> 00:34:17,560
that allows you to ride the dirt trails with the gliding

598
00:34:17,660 --> 00:34:21,960
elegance of a snowboard, get fueled by the grit

599
00:34:22,060 --> 00:34:23,260
and power of a dirt bike.

600
00:34:25,900 --> 00:34:33,960
The MXB is the brainchild of extreme sportsman and inventor, Andy Keel.

601
00:34:35,880 --> 00:34:43,960
With the background in snowboarding and motocross racing, I was able to pick out the most interesting and best parts of those.

602
00:34:43,961 --> 00:34:54,020
I realized that I could create a vehicle that was extremely lightweight, that could be maneuvered like a snowboard, and have the suspension of a motorcycle.

603
00:34:54,021 --> 00:34:58,820
The board rests on two custom-built aluminum wheels.

604
00:34:58,821 --> 00:35:03,320
The frame is composed of tubular aircraft steel.

605
00:35:03,321 --> 00:35:07,960
Coil springs are used to absorb the shock on the rough terrain, and foot platforms allow

606
00:35:07,961 --> 00:35:12,100
for the rider to easily balance on.

607
00:35:12,101 --> 00:35:16,860
The handheld throttle includes a brake lever and a smaller thumb throttle, allowing you

608
00:35:16,861 --> 00:35:19,340
to regulate the speed.

609
00:35:19,341 --> 00:35:22,580
The fuel tank holds enough gas to produce an hour's worth of riding time.

610
00:35:25,300 --> 00:35:30,560
It took Kiel several years to develop a motorized board that could be controlled without a steering wheel.

611
00:35:31,280 --> 00:35:35,080
And his eureka moment came from a trip down the grocery store aisle.

612
00:35:36,240 --> 00:35:40,960
I was pushing a shopping cart in the shopping center, and the wheel was swiveling on the front.

613
00:35:41,280 --> 00:35:47,440
For some reason, I picked the back of the shopping cart up and put a little bit of angle in that caster on the front of that shopping cart,

614
00:35:47,441 --> 00:35:49,020
and it stopped wobbling.

615
00:35:49,160 --> 00:35:52,860
So I realized that, hey, if this thing has got a little bit of angle on it,

616
00:35:52,980 --> 00:35:54,200
it becomes more stable.

617
00:35:54,440 --> 00:35:58,720
So I began to think, how can I have this caster-like design

618
00:35:58,721 --> 00:36:00,580
and not have it in front of the wheel?

619
00:36:00,720 --> 00:36:03,020
And the solution was to sort of reinvent the wheel.

620
00:36:04,120 --> 00:36:06,560
Kiel took the shopping cart's outer caster design

621
00:36:06,561 --> 00:36:09,660
and conformed it within the circumference of the MXB wheel.

622
00:36:10,380 --> 00:36:13,940
This allowed for a 60-degree angle needed for lean to steer,

623
00:36:14,480 --> 00:36:17,040
leaving the driving to the shifts in the rider's weight.

624
00:36:17,440 --> 00:36:22,840
The first one I built in my basement on a budget of about $300.

625
00:36:22,841 --> 00:36:28,560
So I took bicycle parts that I found a BMX bike at a thrift store for $5 and I found

626
00:36:28,561 --> 00:36:33,480
a small two-stroke engine for $60 or $70 and ordered some steel tubing and a welder

627
00:36:33,481 --> 00:36:36,680
and started putting it together.

628
00:36:36,681 --> 00:36:41,260
And the first time I stepped on it to try it, it actually worked.

629
00:36:41,261 --> 00:36:45,620
And according to Keele, the most novice of riders can adapt to his off-roader in a matter

630
00:36:45,621 --> 00:36:46,620
of minutes.

631
00:36:46,621 --> 00:36:50,620
Even my 73-year-old dad has mastered the motocross board.

632
00:36:50,840 --> 00:36:54,760
It didn't take him more than five minutes before he was actually riding it on his own,

633
00:36:54,820 --> 00:36:56,660
and I don't believe he would ever be able to learn how to snowboard.

634
00:36:58,940 --> 00:37:02,220
The MXB can be geared to reach up to 50 miles an hour.

635
00:37:02,840 --> 00:37:08,480
At this speed, many riders would prefer to be strapped in to closer mimic the carving techniques of a snowboard.

636
00:37:09,540 --> 00:37:13,140
This version is equipped with the intuitive release binding system,

637
00:37:13,141 --> 00:37:16,620
which allows the rider to be either free from the board

638
00:37:16,621 --> 00:37:21,000
or to be clamped in securely the way they would on a snowboard

639
00:37:21,001 --> 00:37:24,140
by squeezing this lever on the back of the control handle.

640
00:37:24,320 --> 00:37:27,940
When this lever is closed, the bindings shut down on the boot

641
00:37:27,941 --> 00:37:29,800
and the rider is completely secure.

642
00:37:30,060 --> 00:37:32,200
It has a lot of control the way they do with snowboards.

643
00:37:32,680 --> 00:37:35,840
And then when the rider decides to get off the board,

644
00:37:36,040 --> 00:37:39,260
an intuitive motion allows the rider to open the bindings.

645
00:37:39,261 --> 00:37:41,220
When the handle is released, the bindings open

646
00:37:41,221 --> 00:37:43,980
and the rider is able to get off the board,

647
00:37:44,280 --> 00:37:47,460
unlike with snowboarding where you're always secured to the board.

648
00:37:53,660 --> 00:37:56,040
From the grocery aisle to the muddy paths,

649
00:37:56,340 --> 00:38:00,660
this mean little two-wheeled machine is blazing a whole new trail in the X Games world.

650
00:38:02,540 --> 00:38:04,780
I don't know what the people in the grocery store were thinking

651
00:38:04,781 --> 00:38:07,360
when I was maneuvering that shopping cart around,

652
00:38:07,540 --> 00:38:10,680
but I'm certain they weren't thinking that I would come up with an idea like this from it.

653
00:38:11,220 --> 00:38:20,380
Weird machines and head-turning creations are many times byproducts of the prototyping

654
00:38:20,381 --> 00:38:22,560
business.

655
00:38:22,561 --> 00:38:27,980
Standard Metal Products, a machining business in San Francisco, walks a fine line between

656
00:38:27,981 --> 00:38:30,620
making the practical and the bizarre.

657
00:38:30,621 --> 00:38:35,860
So I'm often asked what I do, and I'll usually just look on the table or around the shop

658
00:38:35,861 --> 00:38:39,220
to see what's in here to answer that question because it's that diverse.

659
00:38:39,221 --> 00:38:44,680
But in the past year, I have repaired a saucepan handle in exchange for two cookies with an

660
00:38:44,681 --> 00:38:46,340
85-year-old woman.

661
00:38:46,341 --> 00:38:51,600
And at the same time, I was prototyping a wind tunnel model for a supersonic wind tunnel

662
00:38:51,601 --> 00:38:54,480
test for NASA.

663
00:38:54,481 --> 00:38:58,980
Kevin Binkert's special set of skills is constantly enlisted to tackle an array of off-kilter

664
00:38:58,981 --> 00:39:06,460
projects, such as the Spirit of America 3, Craig Breedlove's sound barrier-breaking jet

665
00:39:06,461 --> 00:39:07,460
car.

666
00:39:07,461 --> 00:39:11,260
In 1996, Binkert machined the parts for the car's third

667
00:39:11,360 --> 00:39:16,260
incarnation, which reportedly reached speeds up to 675 miles

668
00:39:16,360 --> 00:39:18,700
an hour.

669
00:39:18,800 --> 00:39:21,200
But the prototyping business can also

670
00:39:21,300 --> 00:39:23,400
pose many smaller challenges.

671
00:39:23,500 --> 00:39:26,120
Here's an example of a one of a kind.

672
00:39:26,220 --> 00:39:29,620
This is basically demonstrating that a straight object spinning

673
00:39:29,720 --> 00:39:33,920
through space counterintuitively does not trace out a cylinder.

674
00:39:34,020 --> 00:39:37,460
It actually traces out a hyperbolic shape.

675
00:39:37,461 --> 00:39:42,460
So we prototyped this with the idea that it could be a ride.

676
00:39:42,461 --> 00:39:44,460
Someone would get into this contraption,

677
00:39:44,461 --> 00:39:49,460
and their stray body would go through a curved slot in the wall.

678
00:39:49,461 --> 00:39:52,460
However, SMP's most outrageous invention

679
00:39:52,461 --> 00:39:55,460
was spawned from a vision right out of the subconscious.

680
00:39:55,461 --> 00:39:57,460
This is the flame tornado.

681
00:39:57,461 --> 00:40:00,460
It's a piece that I made in the early 90s

682
00:40:00,461 --> 00:40:03,460
after having a powerful dream of a fire vortex.

683
00:40:03,461 --> 00:40:07,460
And it can be small, or it can burn the roof off the place

684
00:40:07,461 --> 00:40:08,460
if I turn it all the way up.

685
00:40:11,460 --> 00:40:14,460
By using aluminum blades, a 20-gallon propane tank,

686
00:40:14,461 --> 00:40:18,460
a small 1.5-horsepowered engine, and a single mash,

687
00:40:18,461 --> 00:40:22,460
Binkert is able to create this massive tower of fire.

688
00:40:28,460 --> 00:40:30,460
Well, it works a lot like a regular tornado,

689
00:40:30,461 --> 00:40:34,700
a regular tornado where it's pulling air in from the bottom.

690
00:40:34,701 --> 00:40:37,000
So the simple answer to what's happening here

691
00:40:37,001 --> 00:40:38,560
is that we've got a series of blades

692
00:40:38,561 --> 00:40:41,460
that are creating a rotating column of air.

693
00:40:41,461 --> 00:40:44,840
You want laminar flow so there's not a lot of chaotic air.

694
00:40:44,841 --> 00:40:48,120
And as the air rushes in, convection's pulling it up,

695
00:40:48,121 --> 00:40:53,220
and it's a self-perpetuating vortex of flame.

696
00:40:53,221 --> 00:40:58,060
And ideally, the neighbors don't call the fire department.

697
00:40:58,061 --> 00:40:59,100
The result?

698
00:40:59,101 --> 00:41:03,000
A spectacular cyclone of fire, reaching up to 40 feet in height.

699
00:41:08,740 --> 00:41:12,100
The flame tornado has become a fixture at SMP,

700
00:41:12,101 --> 00:41:17,940
but the neighborhood has yet to embrace its entertainment value.

701
00:41:17,941 --> 00:41:20,940
During this demonstration, the San Francisco Fire Department

702
00:41:20,941 --> 00:41:25,980
was called, not the first time for Kevin Baker.

703
00:41:25,981 --> 00:41:31,780
The matter was quickly resolved, but not before Binkert showcased his latest quarter valve, fabricated for the city.

704
00:41:33,140 --> 00:41:38,200
However, the moral of the story, this towering inferno should not be tried at home.

705
00:41:43,980 --> 00:41:47,500
These weird machines symbolize the very nature of who we are.

706
00:41:48,300 --> 00:41:49,580
And mark how far we've come.

707
00:41:49,581 --> 00:41:54,580
Capturing the likes of Jules Verne, R2-D2, Batman,

708
00:41:54,581 --> 00:41:57,580
and even James Bond, while showcasing

709
00:41:57,581 --> 00:42:02,580
American ingenuity at its finest and nuttiest.

